---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import crossings from "../data/crossings.json";
import ships from "../data/ships.json";
import ellis from "../data/ellis.json";
import census from "../data/census.json";
import nyc from "../data/ny-children.json";
const shipOf = new Map(ships.map((s) => [s.slug, s]));
const wave1 = crossings.filter((c) => (c.year || 0) < 1940);
const wave2 = crossings.filter((c) => (c.year || 0) >= 1940);
---
<Base title="The Crossings" eyebrow="Eighteen recorded arrivals and departures"
      dek="This family emigrated twice, in two waves half a century apart, to opposite sides of the world. The first went to America. The second went to Australia. Both left from the same three towns.">

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The first wave — America, 1894–1926</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">Out of <a href={u("/briatico")}>Briatico</a> and
    <a href={u("/scilla")}>Scilla</a>, through Naples, Palermo and Messina, into New York and New
    Orleans. This is the classic Italian emigration and this family was fully part of it. Three of
    them — <P n="Fillipo Melluso" />, <P n="Domenica Anile" /> and <P n="Concetta Anile" /> — came
    off the same ship at New York on <strong>2 August 1906</strong>.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th class="m">Born</th><th>From</th><th>Left</th><th>Arrived</th><th class="m">Date</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{wave1.map((c) => (
          <tr><td><a href={u("/people/" + c.slug)}>{c.who}</a></td>
              <td class="m">{c.born || ""}</td><td>{c.from || "—"}</td>
              <td>{c.left || "—"}</td><td>{c.arrived || "—"}</td>
              <td class="m">{c.arrivedDate}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">One of them went home again. <P n="Maria Rosa Melluso" />
    was <strong>born in New York on 14 October 1907</strong>, left it as a child in 1914, came back
    through the port on 30 June 1926 — and died at Briatico in 1994. An American citizen by birth who
    spent almost her whole life in a Calabrian coastal town, and whose granddaughter's children are
    Australian.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The Ellis Island and New Orleans manifests, read 13 September 2026</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">An age is a birth year, and this family's birth years keep being wrong</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Two men this archive had failed to find in Italian registers left records in America. That
      turned out to be the way in, because <strong>an arrival manifest records an age</strong> — and
      an age converts to a birth year, which is precisely the thing the family tree keeps getting
      wrong. The Statue of Liberty Foundation's Arrival Records Collection is free, needs no account
      for the index, and covers New Orleans as well as Ellis Island.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="rec" style="margin-top:20px">
      <div class="k">CONFIRMED</div>
      <div class="v"><strong><P n="Rocco Arena" /></strong> — Ellis Island, <strong>21 March
      1900</strong>, off the <em>Trave</em> out of Genoa. Last residence <strong>Scilla</strong>,
      married, <strong>aged 39</strong>, indexed year of birth <strong>1861</strong>. Recorded as a
      <strong>US citizen</strong>.</div><div class="w">the tree was right</div>
      <div class="k">CONFIRMED</div>
      <div class="v"><strong><P n="Michele Donato" /></strong> — New Orleans, <strong>1908</strong>,
      off the <em>Liguria</em>, <strong>aged 52</strong> — a birth about <strong>1856</strong>.</div>
      <div class="w">the tree was wrong</div>
      <div class="k">CORRECTED</div>
      <div class="v"><strong><P n="Anna Arena" /></strong> — Ellis Island, <strong>23 May 1899</strong>,
      off the <em>Archimede</em> out of Naples, <strong>aged 6</strong>. The tree puts her crossing in
      <strong>1894</strong>, when she was one year old.</div><div class="w">five years</div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">The Liguria, 22 February 1908 — the image, and
      the oldest question in this archive</h3>
      <p>The index gave <P n="Michele Donato" />'s age and nothing else. <strong>The manifest page
      itself gives five more things, and one of them closes a question that has been open since the
      first week.</strong></p>
      <blockquote style="margin:1.1rem 0">S.S. <strong>Liguria</strong>, sailing from
      <strong>Palermo</strong>, <strong>22 February 1908</strong> — <em>List or Manifest of Alien
      Passengers for the United States</em></blockquote>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
        <div class="k">line 15</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Donato, Michele</strong> — 52 — married — <strong>sailor</strong> —
        Italian, South Calabria, <strong>Scilla</strong><br />
        <span class="small">nearest relative: <strong>"Wife Maria Sofi, Via Grotte, Scilla"</strong>
        · destination <strong>Quincy, Florida</strong></span></div><div class="w">no. 130</div>
        <div class="k">line 16</div>
        <div class="v"><strong>Polistena, Giovanni</strong> — 24 — single —
        <strong>sailor</strong> — Italian, South Calabria, <strong>Scilla</strong><br />
        <span class="small">nearest relative: <strong>"Father Antonino Polistena and mother Giovanna
        Romano, Scilla"</strong> · destination <strong>Quincy, Florida</strong></span></div>
        <div class="w">no. 131</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>They are on consecutive lines.</strong>
      <P n="Michele Donato" /> and <P n="Giovanni Polistena" /> crossed the Atlantic together in
      February 1908, bound for the same town in Florida — and about eight years later Giovanni
      married Michele's daughter <P n="Nunnziata Donato" />.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.6rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">Romano, not Pontillo — settled</h3>
      <p>The family tree gives Giovanni Polistena's mother as <strong>Giovanna Pontillo</strong>.
      <a href={u("/scilla")}>Act 206 of 1883</a> says <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong>. This archive
      has carried that as an open conflict for days, noting each time that <em>Pontillo</em> is a
      real Scilla surname and the tree might yet be right.</p>
      <p><strong>It is not.</strong> A United States immigration manifest, filled in from Giovanni's
      own statement <strong>twenty-five years after his birth and half a world from Scilla</strong>,
      names his mother <strong>Giovanna Romano</strong>. Two independent records, two continents, one
      answer.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px">The father's name moves a little too, and towards the
      tree. The 1883 act calls him <strong>Camillo Antonio Polistena</strong>; the tree calls him
      <strong>Antonio</strong>; his son in 1908 calls him <strong>Antonino</strong>. All three are
      the same man, and the tree's version was never the problem.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem" class="small">Louisiana, New Orleans Passenger Lists, 1820–1945
      (NARA T905), roll 19, image 763 — FamilySearch <code>3:1:33S7-95CH-L8S</code>. The page was
      read by stitching its DeepZoom tiles at native resolution, 4048 × 4097.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-bottom:.6rem">Michele Donato: two independent witnesses to 1856</h3>
      <p>The tree puts his birth at Scilla in <strong>1851</strong>. His daughter's birth act makes
      him <strong>36 in July 1892</strong>. This manifest makes him <strong>52 in 1908</strong>.
      <strong>Both give about 1856, and neither supports 1851</strong> — and the manifest image above makes a third — which is exactly why the
      1851 index had no Donato in it. It is the only New Orleans arrival among forty-nine Michele
      Donato records in the collection, and it matches the port and the year the tree already
      claimed.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem">It also settles a doubt this archive raised three days ago. Having
      found no Donato in the Scilla birth indexes for 1851, 1856 or 1859, the working hypothesis was
      that <em>the surname arrived at Scilla late and Michele was born elsewhere</em>.
      <strong>Fifteen Donato gave Scilla as their last residence at Ellis Island</strong> between
      1893 and 1921. They were unquestionably a Scilla family. Where Michele was <em>born</em> is
      still open — but "the Donato are not really of Scilla" is not the answer.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">Rocco Arena: the conflict resolves, against
      the tree's parents rather than its dates</h3>
      <p>This archive has spent three days on a single contradiction. The tree makes
      <P n="Rocco Arena" /> — born 1861 — the son of <P n="Orazio Arena" /> and Giovana Zirilli.
      <a href={u("/scilla")}>Act 228 of the 1861 Scilla register</a> says <strong>Francesco Antonio
      Arena</strong>, <em>marinaro</em>, and <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong>, and 1861 holds
      <strong>exactly one</strong> Rocco Arena. Either the parents were wrong or the year was.</p>
      <p><strong>Three independent strands now point the same way.</strong></p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
        <div class="k">The register</div>
        <div class="v">1861 contains one Rocco Arena, and he is Francesco Antonio's</div>
        <div class="w">atto 228</div>
        <div class="k">The trade</div>
        <div class="v">Rocco is a <em>marinaro</em> in 1893; Francesco Antonio is a <em>marinaro</em>
        in 1861. These trades passed from father to son.</div><div class="w">atto 22 / 1893</div>
        <div class="k">The manifest</div>
        <div class="v">A Rocco Arena of Scilla, married, <strong>aged 39 in March 1900</strong> — born
        about <strong>1861</strong>, from a source that has never seen this family tree. The image
        adds his trade: <strong>Sailor</strong>, matching the <em>marinaro</em> of 1893.</div>
        <div class="w">the Trave</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">The tree's <strong>year is right</strong>. What is almost certainly
      wrong is the <strong>parentage</strong> — and Orazio Arena and Giovanna Zirilli, who are
      <a href={u("/scilla")}>documented twice over</a> as a real Scilla couple, are simply a
      different household that the tree attached him to.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
        <p><strong>Added later the same day, and it cuts the other way.</strong> The 1858 Tavola was
        opened looking for Giovanni Arena. He is not in it — but <strong>act 60 of 1858 is a
        <em>Rocco</em> Arena</strong>, born 23 April, son of <strong>Santo Arena and Giovanna
        Gioffrè</strong>. The claim above is still literally true — <em>1861</em> holds exactly one
        Rocco Arena — but the useful version of it, that there is only one Rocco Arena of about the
        right age to choose from, <strong>is not true any more</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.6rem">The arithmetic still favours 1861. A man born 20 December 1861 is
        <strong>38</strong> in March 1900; the 1858 boy would be <strong>41</strong>; the manifest
        says <strong>39</strong>. And the tree, independently, says 1861. But "three strands agree"
        was a cleaner sentence yesterday than it is today, and the archive would rather say so than
        leave the cleaner sentence standing.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>One honest caveat.</strong> Manifest ages are
      approximate and the indexed "year of birth" is derived from the age, not copied from a
      document. A man born on 20 December 1861 would be <strong>38</strong> in March 1900, not 39 —
      so this is a year loose. It does not pin the date. What it does do is rule out the alternative
      the archive was entertaining, that Rocco's birth was years away from 1861 and hiding in another
      volume. <strong>The chart still draws the tree as it stands</strong>, because this archive does
      not rewrite what it was given on the balance of evidence — it records the balance instead.</p>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">Who took a six-year-old across the Atlantic</h3>
      <p>Anna Arena crossed on the <em>Archimede</em> on 23 May 1899, aged six. On the
      <strong>same film, the same frame, the line immediately above hers</strong>, is
      <strong>Giavanni Arena, 45, married, of Scilla</strong>. She travelled with him, not with her
      father — who arrived ten months later, and as an American citizen. Her brother Antonio had
      been born in New York in 1895.</p>
      <p><strong>So the order runs backwards from what the tree implies.</strong> Rocco Arena was
      in America first and for long enough to naturalise; his six-year-old daughter was brought over
      in 1899 by another Arena; and the family later went home to Scilla, where Anna married Giuseppe
      Arena and their son Rocco was born in 1924.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
        <p><strong>The image settles who he was, and it does so twice.</strong> Line 4 of the
        manifest does not begin with a surname. It begins with the word <strong>"Niece"</strong> —
        <em>"Niece, Anna Arena, 6, occupation none"</em>. And in the column asking whether the
        passenger goes to join a relative, line 3 reads
        <strong>"brother Rocco, N. York, Mulberry St"</strong>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>Giovanni Arena is Rocco Arena's brother</strong> — a
        sibling the family tree does not have — and <strong>Rocco was living on Mulberry Street</strong>
        in May 1899. The document states both relationships outright; nothing here is inferred.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.6rem" class="small">His age, 45, matches the <strong>Arena Giovanni born
        at Scilla on 20 April 1854</strong> to the day. One loose end: that act names the mother
        <em>Rosa</em> Arlotta where Rocco's names her <em>Serafina</em> Arlotta. The father in both is
        written <em>"Ant.º"</em>, exactly as the 1852 act writes Francesco Antonio.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">Five lines of one manifest: the Luisiana,
      2 August 1906</h3>
      <p>The tree records three people arriving at New York on <strong>2 August 1906</strong> and
      names no ship. It was the <strong><em>Luisiana</em></strong>, and they were not three people
      but <strong>five, on five consecutive lines</strong> of one page:</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
        <div class="k">line 25</div><div class="v">Teresa Anile, 20, married</div>
        <div class="w">not in the tree</div>
        <div class="k">line 26</div><div class="v"><P n="Concetta Anile" />, 21</div>
        <div class="w">the tree says 18</div>
        <div class="k">line 27</div><div class="v"><strong><P n="Domenica Anile" />, 26</strong></div>
        <div class="w">exact</div>
        <div class="k">line 28</div><div class="v"><strong><P n="Fillipo Melluso" />, 3</strong> —
        indexed as <em>"Foloppo Melluso Anile"</em></div><div class="w">her son</div>
        <div class="k">line 29</div><div class="v">Francesco Anile, 1</div>
        <div class="w">not in the tree</div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Domenica Anile's birth is given in the tree as
      <strong>20 February 1880</strong>, which makes her <strong>twenty-six</strong> on the day —
      and the manifest says twenty-six. The three-year-old on the next line is her son by Domenico
      Melluso, and the transcriber ran the child's surname into his mother's, which is how a
      <strong>Filippo Melluso</strong> came to be indexed as <em>Foloppo Melluso Anile</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>Two people on that page are not in the family tree at all</strong> — Teresa Anile,
      20, and the one-year-old Francesco. A family of five crossed; the tree remembers three.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px">There is a <strong>second Filippo Melluso of Briatico
      on the same ship</strong>, aged thirteen, four lines and two frames earlier. He is not the
      tree's man — the tree's is the three-year-old — and he is recorded here so that no one later
      merges them on the strength of a name and a date.</p>
      <div class="note" style="margin-top:18px">
        <p><strong>The image has now been read, and it names the man they were going to.</strong>
        The five lines are braced together and the <em>going to join</em> column gives one address for
        all of them: <strong>"Domenico Melluso, 2173 First Ave, N.Y."</strong> The relationship words
        across the group are <em>brother</em> (or <em>mother</em>), <strong>husband</strong>,
        <strong>father</strong>, <strong>father</strong>, <em>cousin</em>.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.6rem">So <P n="Domenica Anile" /> was sailing to her
        <strong>husband</strong> — which the tree already says, and the manifest now confirms — and
        the two small boys to their <strong>father</strong>. The name column carries only
        <strong>two written surnames</strong>, <em>Anile</em> against the women and <em>Melluso</em>
        against the children, with ditto braces. <strong>That is exactly how the index came to hand a
        Melluso child an Anile surname.</strong> The one-year-old on line 29, indexed as "Francesco
        Anile", is <strong>Francesco Melluso</strong> — Domenica's younger son, and not in the family
        tree.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>And the address places them.</strong> 2173 First Avenue is
        <strong>East Harlem</strong>, not Little Italy. The Briatico people were settling a mile and a
        half uptown from where the Scilla Arena were living on Mulberry Street — two branches of one
        family in two different Italian New Yorks, seven years apart.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:.6rem" class="small"><strong>One caveat.</strong> The exact line-by-line
        alignment of the relationship column is not certain, because the brace spans several rows.
        The destination and the husband are.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:1.8rem;margin-bottom:.6rem">The Polistena and the Donato were already
      together in 1903</h3>
      <p>On the <em>Sicilian Prince</em>, 12 August 1903, line 1 is <strong>Annunziata Polistena,
      17</strong>, and line 2 is <strong>Giovanni Donato, 16</strong> — both of Scilla, side by side
      on the same page. Further down the same manifest, lines 28 and 29, are <strong>Giuseppe
      Polistena, 30</strong>, and <strong>Maria Polistena, 27</strong>, a married couple.</p>
      <p><strong>Giovanni Polistena did not marry Nunziata Donato until about 1916.</strong> Thirteen
      years earlier two teenagers from those families were crossing the Atlantic on consecutive lines
      of one ship's manifest. And in 1901 on the <em>Neustria</em>, Pasquale Donato and Lorenjo Costa
      of Scilla are two lines apart — Costa being <P n="Generosa Costa" />'s surname. These were not
      three families who happened to marry; they were neighbours who moved together.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Arrived</th><th>Port</th><th class="m">Age</th><th class="m">→ born</th><th>Ship</th><th class="m">Line</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {ellis.rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{r.name}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{r.arrived}</td>
              <td>{r.port}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.age}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.birthYear}</td>
              <td><em>{r.ship}</em></td>
              <td class="m">{r.line} · {r.film}/{r.frame}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:14px">Statue of Liberty &amp; Ellis Island Foundation, Arrival
    Records Collection — <em>New York, Passenger Arrival Lists (Ellis Island), 1892–1925</em> and
    <em>Louisiana, New Orleans Passenger Lists, 1820–1945</em> (NARA T905). Every row carries a
    FamilySearch ark for the manifest page itself. <strong>The Liguria page has now been read in
    full</strong> — see above. The remaining images are still to do.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The 1900 United States Census — Manhattan, Ward 6</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">One line, and a whole Scilla household under a New York roof</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>A manifest gives you one person at a time. <strong>A census sheet gives you everyone in the
      building.</strong> The 1899 manifest sent <P n="Giovanni Arena" />'s brother to Mulberry Street;
      Mulberry Street is in Ward 6; and here, on sheet 17A, line 16, household 325, is
      <P n="Rocco Arena" />.</p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:18px">
        {census[0].memberList.map((m, i) => (
          <>
            <div class="k">{i === 0 ? "head" : (i < 4 ? "family" : "also here")}</div>
            <div class="v">{m}</div>
            <div class="w">{i === 0 ? "line 16" : ""}</div>
          </>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>It confirms the family tree on four points at once</strong>
      — the 1892 arrival, Generosa Costa as his wife, Anna as his daughter, and Antonio born in New
      York — and it adds two things the tree does not have. He had been <strong>married ten
      years</strong>, so <strong>Rocco Arena and Generosa Costa married in 1890</strong>. And he gave
      his own birth as <strong>September 1861</strong>, where act 228 of 1861 says 20 December.
      Census months are self-reported and three months adrift is ordinary; <strong>the year
      agrees</strong>, which is the part that was ever in doubt.</p>
      <p><strong>The other five names are the finding.</strong> Living with them: a
      <strong>Costa</strong>, which is Generosa's surname; an <strong>Arlotta</strong>, which is
      Rocco's mother's surname; and <strong>three more Arena</strong> — Domenico 18, John 42 and
      Rafael 17. This is not a household, it is a piece of Scilla transplanted into one tenement.
      <strong>John Arena, 42</strong>, is all but certainly the brother Giovanni who had brought
      six-year-old Anna over the year before. And <strong>Lorenzo Costa, 24</strong>, is all but
      certainly the <em>Lorenjo Costa, 26, of Scilla</em> who came off the <em>Neustria</em> on
      13 March 1901, two lines from a Donato.</p>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:.8rem">United States Census, 1900 — Borough of Manhattan,
      Election District 5, New York City Ward 6, ED 117, New York County; NARA T623. FamilySearch
      <code>1:1:MSJ5-T7D</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">New York, New York City Births and Deaths</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Four children born in Manhattan, three of whom the tree never knew</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The family tree has Rocco Arena and Generosa Costa going to America and coming home. It does
      not have what happened in between.</p>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Born</th><th>In the tree?</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {nyc.map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <td><strong>{c.name}</strong></td>
                <td class="m">{c.born}</td>
                <td>{c.inTree === "yes"
                  ? <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">yes</span>
                  : <strong>no</strong>}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><strong>Sarafina Arena, born 14 August 1898, died on 30 January
      1900</strong> and was buried the next day, aged seventeen months. That is why she is not in the
      census taken that June, and it is why nobody remembered her.</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>And her name is evidence.</strong> Act 228 of
      1861 gives Rocco Arena's mother as <strong>Serafina Arlotta</strong> — the parentage this
      archive spent four days arguing about. In New York in 1898, Rocco named a daughter
      <strong>Serafina</strong>. It is not proof, and it is the third separate thing to point the
      same way: the sibling pair in the register, the <em>Arlotta</em> lodging with them in 1900, and
      now a dead child's name.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The second wave — Australia, 1949–1961</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.8rem 0 1.2rem">Every single one of these sailed from
    <strong>Genoa</strong>. That is not a coincidence of record-keeping: post-war assisted migration to
    Australia was run out of the northern ports under the 1951 Italian–Australian migration agreement,
    and a Calabrian or Sicilian family travelled the length of the country to board.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th class="m">Born</th><th>From</th><th>Left</th><th>Arrived</th><th class="m">Date</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{wave2.map((c) => (
          <tr><td><a href={u("/people/" + c.slug)}>{c.who}</a></td>
              <td class="m">{c.born || ""}</td><td>{c.from || "—"}</td>
              <td>{c.left || "—"}</td><td>{c.arrived || "—"}</td>
              <td class="m">{c.arrivedDate}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">10 September 2026 — the National Archives passenger index</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Nine of them now have a ship</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.9rem 0 1.4rem">The NAA passenger arrivals index, 1898–1972, is
    arranged by <strong>voyage</strong>: one item is one ship on one date, and every passenger aboard
    shares the item number. Searching it by name and date turned nine of these eighteen crossings from
    a date into a named vessel.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th class="m">Date</th><th>Ship</th><th>From</th><th>Arrived</th><th class="m">NAA item</th><th>Confidence</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{ships.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td><a href={u("/people/" + s.slug)}>{s.name}</a></td>
            <td class="m">{s.naaDate}</td>
            <td><strong><em>{s.ship.charAt(0) + s.ship.slice(1).toLowerCase()}</em></strong></td>
            <td>{s.naaFrom}</td>
            <td>{s.naaPort}</td>
            <td class="m"><a href={`https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/scripts/AutoSearch.asp?Number=${s.itemId}`} rel="noopener">{s.itemId}</a></td>
            <td>{s.confidence === "documented"
              ? <span class="chip doc">documented</span>
              : <span class="chip inf">inferred</span>}</td>
          </tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lab" style="margin-top:32px">Where the index corrects the tree</h3>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:14px">
      {ships.filter((s) => s.note).map((s) => (
        <p class="qn" style="max-width:88ch"><b>{s.name}</b> — {s.note}</p>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="note" style="margin-top:26px">The most instructive of these is the
    <em>Surriento</em>. The tree has <P n="Venera Cavallaro" /> and <P n="Luciano Cavallaro" /> arriving
    at <strong>Brisbane on 6 October 1953</strong>; the index has them at <strong>Fremantle on
    23 September 1953</strong>, on one item. Both are true. A ship out of Italy made Fremantle first
    and worked round the coast, and a family remembers the day they got off — not the day the ship
    first touched Australia. <strong>Neither date is wrong, and the archive keeps both.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What the order of arrival says</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><strong>June 1949</strong> — <P n="Angela Polistena" /> and <P n="Antonino Polistena" /> reach
      Brisbane together. <strong>October 1949</strong> — <P n="Sebastiano Mazza" /> reaches Fremantle,
      alone. <strong>May 1950</strong> — <P n="Michael Antonio Polistena" />. <strong>March 1951</strong> —
      <P n="Domenica Prostamo" /> follows with a son not yet three years old. <strong>1953</strong> —
      <P n="Rocco Arena" />, and the Cavallaro. <strong>1957</strong> — <P n="Francessco Prostamo" />,
      aged fifty-six, last of all.</p>
      <p>That is the standard shape of chain migration and it can be read straight off the dates: a
      man goes first, works, and sends for his wife and child eighteen months later; the wife's
      relatives follow; the grandfather comes last, when there is somewhere for him to arrive. What
      the archive cannot yet say is <em>which ships</em>. Not one of these eighteen crossings names a
      vessel. <a href={u("/open-questions")}>That is the first thing to fix →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>And a third destination</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.8rem">A branch of the Briatico Prostamo went to
    <strong>Buenos Aires</strong>. The archive holds twenty-three Argentine events — births from 1903
    through to 1962, and the death of <P n="Giuseppe Prostamo" /> there. No crossing is recorded for
    any of them, so how and when they went is unknown. Argentina took more Calabrians than Australia
    ever did; this branch is a normal part of that, and it is the least documented thing in the
    archive.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
